Why vendored rather than depended on:

  The crates.io release (0.1.0) targets egui 0.31 and upstream master targets
  0.34; mogeung is on 0.35. A git dependency would resolve egui from upstream's
  own workspace, so two incompatible egui versions would end up in the tree and
  the widget's types would not match ours.

Local changes:

  - the egui version, in Cargo.toml
  - src/view.rs, TerminalView::process_input — upstream gates *all* input on
    `layout.contains_pointer()`, so keyboard input dies whenever the mouse
    moves off the pane, with nothing on screen to say why. Keyboard is now
    gated on focus alone; mouse events still require the pointer inside.
    Marked in the source with `LOCAL CHANGE (mogeung)`.
  - src/view.rs, process_mouse_wheel — upstream always emitted `Scroll`, so an
    application that enabled mouse reporting (tmux with `mouse on`) never
    received the wheel and an alt-screen app got arrow keys instead. The wheel
    is now reported as mouse buttons 64/65 when MOUSE_MODE is active, one
    press-only report per line, with Shift bypassing reporting per convention.
    Marked with `LOCAL CHANGE (mogeung)`.
  - src/backend/mod.rs, the pty_event_subscription thread — upstream looped
    `if let Ok(event) = recv()`, which busy-spins at 100% CPU forever once the
    channel's senders are gone; dropping a TerminalBackend (every session
    switch in the Terminal pane) is exactly that, so each switch leaked one
    spinning thread. Found live: 38 leaked threads pinning every core after
    two hours of use. A closed channel now ends the thread, and a hung-up app
    receiver ends it instead of panicking. Marked with
    `LOCAL CHANGE (mogeung)`.

  - src/view.rs, the primary mouse button — upstream forwarded clicks to the
    application whenever it enabled any mouse reporting, and forwarded drags
    only under mode 1003. Claude Code always enables mouse reporting (tmux
    passes the mode through), so left-drag selection was dead in exactly the
    sessions this pane exists for, and clicks fell into whatever tmux was
    doing (copy-mode selections landing in tmux's buffer, unreachable from
    the system clipboard — egui-term speaks no OSC 52). The left button now
    always drives local selection (`forward_primary_to_app` is the switch),
    a drag always extends it, and releasing copies the selection to the
    system clipboard, PuTTY-style. `selection_text()` on the backend reads
    the live terminal via alacritty's `selection_to_string` — the old
    `selectable_content()` read the render cache, which misses a selection
    made the same frame, and dropped newlines. Ctrl+Shift+C uses the same
    path. Marked with `LOCAL CHANGE (mogeung)`.

  - src/backend/settings.rs and src/backend/mod.rs, `BackendSettings::env` —
    upstream offered no way to set the child's environment, and
    alacritty_terminal sets no `TERM` of its own, so the child inherited the
    window process's. A window started from a launcher or the tray has no
    `TERM` at all, and the tmux inside then died on every attach with
    `open terminal failed: terminal does not support clear`. The extra
    environment now reaches `tty::Options::env`. Marked with
    `LOCAL CHANGE (mogeung)`.

To update: clone upstream, copy src/ over this one, re-apply the changes above
(grep for `LOCAL CHANGE`), run the workspace tests.
